<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.4: Quantities that Change Exponentially</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01373</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.4: Quantities that Change Exponentially</md:title>
    <md:uuid>64d372ce-e8ca-421c-91a1-5a8062a48cf4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">
        <media
          alt="Scatter plot showing a steep decline in value (dollars) over time (years) from about $700 at year 1 to near $0 by year 10, indicating rapid decrease followed by stabilization.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_09_01.svg" />
        </media>

      </para>
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">This graph represents one of the following descriptions. Which one?</para>
          <para id="para-00003">a. A phone loses <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its value every year after purchase; the relationship between the number of years
            since purchasing the phone and the value of the phone.</para>
          <para id="para-00004">b. The number of stores a company has tripled approximately every 5
            years; the relationship between the number of years and the number of stores.</para>
          <para id="para-00005">c. A camera loses <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its value every year after purchase; the relationship between the number of years
            since purchasing the camera and the value of the camera.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00007">A camera loses <m:math display="inline">
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mfrac>
                  <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                </m:mfrac>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:math>
            of its value every year after purchase; the relationship between the number of years
            since purchasing the camera and the value of the camera.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Explain how you know the graph represents the description you chose.</para>

        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00009">Your answers may vary, but here are some examples:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>The graph cannot represent Description A because the phone is retaining only <m:math
                display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              of its value, which is less than half, and the vertical coordinate of the second point
              on the graph is more than half of the vertical intercept.</item>
            <item>If the camera loses <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              of its value each year, then its value is <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              that of the previous year. The vertical intercept seems to be 1,200, and <m:math
                display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mfrac>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mn>5</m:mn>
                  </m:mfrac>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              of 1,200 is about 700, which is roughly the vertical coordinate of the second point.</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>